# 0010-lock-blank-timer-20s -- metadata and post-conditions.
#
# Parsed by omarchy-deck-apply-patches. Format: `key: value`, one per line,
# `#` comments, blank lines ignored. An UNKNOWN key is a hard error -- a
# post-condition that was silently ignored because of a typo is worse than no
# post-condition, because it also buys false confidence.
#
# WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS
#
# The lock screen blanks the panel ~5 s after locking, which on a handheld
# reads as "it died". docs/PROGRESS.md §5.24 measured ~5-6 s;
# docs/findings/T9-lock-wake-and-blank-timing.md §3 traced it to a hardcoded
# QML Timer, NOT to Hyprland's DPMS timeout and NOT to any shell.json key --
# there is no config knob for this value upstream. §5.2 of that finding is the
# source of the one-line diff.
#
# WHY IT IS A PATCH AND NOT A `sed`
#
# Upstream retuned this exact literal three times in two months:
#   35a6940 (2026-06-20) 30000 -> 3000   "Just 3 seconds of screen on after lock"
#   af82848 (2026-07-04)  3000 -> 5000   "Give it a little more time"
# A sed would match nothing on the next retune, exit 0, and lose the fix in
# silence. The context lines below turn that same event into a reject, which is
# the entire point of this mechanism.
#
# EXPECT TO REBASE THIS ROUGHLY MONTHLY. shell/plugins/lock/Service.qml is the
# single BREAKS US row of docs/findings/T9-delta-classification.md and had 17
# commits in three months. That is a known, accepted, and *loud* cost.
#
# NOT IN SCOPE: patching out recoverStrandedLock(), which lives in this same
# file. Operator decision §5.25 #1 chose masking + above_lock instead. It is a
# behavioural change to a security-relevant path and needs its own patch, its
# own approval and its own hardware gate (T12 finding §6.3).

requirement: docs/PROGRESS.md §5.24a row 2 -- the lock screen must keep the panel lit ~20 s, not ~2-5 s
description: shell/plugins/lock/Service.qml idleBlankTimer.interval 5000 -> 20000
target: shell/plugins/lock/Service.qml

# The new value must be there exactly once...
assert_count: shell/plugins/lock/Service.qml|^    interval: 20000$|1
# ...and the old value must be gone. Both directions matter: a patch that
# lands on the wrong one of several similar hunks would satisfy the first
# assertion alone. `interval: 5000` occurs exactly once in the file at the
# RUNTIME pin 6d7826d (verified), so 0 is the right expectation after the
# patch.
assert_count: shell/plugins/lock/Service.qml|^    interval: 5000$|0
# The file is loaded by a QML engine minutes-to-hours later, on a device with
# no terminal. qmllint exits 0 on the correctly patched file despite its
# unresolvable qs.* imports, and 255 on broken QML (both verified, T12 finding
# §7). It ships with qt6-declarative, which quickshell depends on.
qmllint: shell/plugins/lock/Service.qml
